\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {20.0.1}{\ignorespaces The sum segment tree over \(A\) \emph  {after} the one lazy act \(\text  {range-add }[2..5]\mathrel {+}= 3\). Green nodes are the two root-paths the walk touched on its way down and pulled on its way up; the orange box \(t_9\) is the \textbf  {\textcolor {deepblue}{pending tag}} -- its interval \([3,4]\) was fully covered, so the update was folded into its aggregate (\(9\to 15\)) and \emph  {stashed} as \(+3\) without descending. The two orange leaves \(A_3,A_4\) still read their OLD values \(4,5\): they are stale by exactly \(+3\), the difference now parked in \(t_9\)'s tag, to be pushed only if someone later descends into \(t_9\). Cross-check: the root \(t_1=61 = 49 + 3\cdot 4\) (four slots, three each), while \(t_3=25\) and every node off the touched paths is \emph  {unchanged} from Ch.19's grid. The leaves \(t_{16..31}\) equal \(A\) and the untouched structure matches Figure~\ref {fig:c13-tree} -- only the internal nodes on the update paths differ, because they are interval aggregates carrying the deferred work.}}{1214}{figure.caption.214}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c15-tree}{{20.0.1}{1214}{The sum segment tree over \(A\) \emph {after} the one lazy act \(\text {range-add }[2..5]\mathrel {+}= 3\). Green nodes are the two root-paths the walk touched on its way down and pulled on its way up; the orange box \(t_9\) is the \term {pending tag} -- its interval \([3,4]\) was fully covered, so the update was folded into its aggregate (\(9\to 15\)) and \emph {stashed} as \(+3\) without descending. The two orange leaves \(A_3,A_4\) still read their OLD values \(4,5\): they are stale by exactly \(+3\), the difference now parked in \(t_9\)'s tag, to be pushed only if someone later descends into \(t_9\). Cross-check: the root \(t_1=61 = 49 + 3\cdot 4\) (four slots, three each), while \(t_3=25\) and every node off the touched paths is \emph {unchanged} from Ch.19's grid. The leaves \(t_{16..31}\) equal \(A\) and the untouched structure matches \figref {c13-tree} -- only the internal nodes on the update paths differ, because they are interval aggregates carrying the deferred work}{figure.caption.214}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {20.0.2}{\ignorespaces The chapter's six-state taxonomy, read top to bottom as an escalation. Rows~1--2 are the machine (the pending tag, push-on-demand); row~3 is the \emph  {composition law} -- what happens when two updates meet at one node; row~4 is the update that made a BIT give up (assignment); row~5 moves the whole mechanism onto a tree's Euler array; row~6 is the \emph  {boundary} -- the updates that no single tag can represent, which belong to segment-tree beats (Ch.23) or, if the array never changes, to the sparse table (Ch.21). Every solved problem sits on exactly one row: CF~295A on rows~1--2, CF~817F on row~3, CF~915E on row~4, CF~620E on row~5, and the Summit spanning rows~3--4 (a tag you compose while walking the operations in reverse).}}{1216}{figure.caption.215}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c15-taxonomy}{{20.0.2}{1216}{The chapter's six-state taxonomy, read top to bottom as an escalation. Rows~1--2 are the machine (the pending tag, push-on-demand); row~3 is the \emph {composition law} -- what happens when two updates meet at one node; row~4 is the update that made a BIT give up (assignment); row~5 moves the whole mechanism onto a tree's Euler array; row~6 is the \emph {boundary} -- the updates that no single tag can represent, which belong to segment-tree beats (Ch.23) or, if the array never changes, to the sparse table (Ch.21). Every solved problem sits on exactly one row: CF~295A on rows~1--2, CF~817F on row~3, CF~915E on row~4, CF~620E on row~5, and the Summit spanning rows~3--4 (a tag you compose while walking the operations in reverse)}{figure.caption.215}{}}
